Kg</FONT></TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0><tr><td height=12></td></tr></table><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0><TR><TD width=20></TD><TD><b>  Explanation：</b>The proportion of body fat to body weight in a normal and healthy person is: 14% to 20% for men and 17% to 24% for women.</TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0><tr><td height=20></td></tr></table><TABLE width=590 align=center   border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0><tr><td height=12></td></tr></table><TABLE align=center cellSpacing=0 cellPadding=0 width=600 bgColor=#c0c0c0 border=1><TR><TD align=middle width=124 bgColor=#c0c0c0><B><FONT size=2>  property</FONT></B></TD><TD align=middle width=150><B><FONT size=2>Low standard</FONT></B></TD><TD align=middle width=100><B><FONT size=2>Standard</FONT></B></TD><TD align=middle width=226><B><FONT size=2>Exceed the standard</FONT></B></TD></TR>   </TABLE><TABLE align=center cellSpacing=0 cellPadding=0 width=600 border=0><TR><TD width=100>&nbsp;<WBR></TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle  width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD></TD></TR><TR>  <TD width=100>&nbsp;<WBR></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>5%</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>8%</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>11%</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT  size=2>14%</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>17%</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>20%</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>23%</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>  26%</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>29%</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>30%</FONT></TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE align=center cellSpacing=0 cellPadding=0 width=600 border=0><TR><TD width=125 align=center><FONT size=2>⑤Body fat percentage</FONT></TD><TD align=left width=475 colSpan=10 valign=center><img src=Mark.jpg width=MyValue18
 height=15 border=0/>&nbsp;<FONT size=3>MyValue19
 %</FONT></TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0><tr><td height=12></td></tr></table><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0><TR><TD width=20></TD><TD><b>Explanation： </b>Body fat percentage refers to the ratio of body fat content to body weight.<br/>The normal fat content for men is 14% to 20%, 20% to 25% is considered overweight, and over 25% is considered obese.<br/>The normal fat content for women is 17% to 24%, 25% to 30% is considered overweight, and over 30% is regarded as obese.</TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0    cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0><tr><td height=20></td></tr></table><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0><tr><td height=12></td></tr></table><TABLE align=center cellSpacing=0 cellPadding=0 width=600    bgColor=#c0c0c0 border=1><TR><TD align=middle width=124 bgColor=#c0c0c0><B><FONT size=2>property</FONT></B></TD><TD align=middle width=150><B><FONT size=2>Low standard</FONT></B></TD><TD align=middle width=100><B><FONT size=2>Standard</FONT>   </B></TD><TD align=middle width=226><B><FONT size=2>Exceed the standard</FONT></B></TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE align=center cellSpacing=0 cellPadding=0 width=600 border=0><TR><TD width=100>&nbsp;<WBR></TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD>        <TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD>   <TD align=middle width=50>|</TD><TD align=middle width=50>|</TD></TD></TR><TR><TD width=100>&nbsp;<WBR></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>0.65</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>0.70</FONT></TD><TD      align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>0.75</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>0.80</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>0.85</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>0.90</FONT></TD><TD        align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>0.95</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>1.00</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>1.05</FONT></TD><TD align=middle width=50><FONT size=2>1.10</FONT></TD></TR>     </TABLE><TABLE align=center cellSpacing=0 cellPadding=0 width=600 border=0><TR><TD width=125 align=center><FONT size=2>⑥Abdominal fat ratio</FONT></TD><TD align=left width=475 colSpan=10 valign=center><img src=Mark.jpg width=MyValue20
 height=15 border=0/>&nbsp;<FONT size=3>MyValue21
</FONT></TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0><tr><td height=12></td></tr></table><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0><TR><TD width=20></TD><TD><b>Explanation： </b>This is also called the waist-to-hip ratio, which is the ratio of waist circumference to height. Waist-to-hip ratio（WHR）=W（cm）/H（cm）。</TD></TR><TR><TD width=20></TD><TD><TABLE align=center border=0><TR align=center><TD width=80>Waist-to-hip ratio</TD><TD width=60>Normal</TD><TD width=100>  Lumbar obesity</TD><TD width=100>Gluteal obesity</TD></TR><TR align=center><TD>Male</TD><TD><0.9</TD><TD>>1.0</TD><TD><1.0</TD></TR><TR align=center><TD>female</TD><TD><0.8</TD><TD>>0.85</TD><TD><0.85</TD></TR></TABLE></TD></TR></TABLE>    <TABLE width=590 align=center border=0><TR><TD><Font size=3><STRONG>
3、Nutritional judgment</STRONG></Font></TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE class=table cellSpacing=0 cellPadding=3 width=590 align=center border=0><TR class=td align=middle bgcolor=B3D9ED height=28><TD class=td colspan=2><STRONG>Nutritional judgment </STRONG></TD></TR><TR class=td align=left bgcolor=EBF5FB height=32><TD class=td align=middle>Degree of obesity(ODB)</TD><TD class=td align=middle>MyValue22
%</TD></TR><TR class=td align=left bgcolor=EBF5FB height=32><TD class=td align=middle>Body mass parameters(BMI)</TD><TD class=td align=middle>MyValue23
Kg/M<sup>2</sup></TD></TR><TR class=td align=left bgcolor=EBF5FB height=32><TD class=td align=middle>Basal metabolism(BMR)</TD><TD class=td align=middle>MyValue24
kcal</TD></TR><TR class=td align=left bgcolor=EBF5FB height=32><TD class=td align=middle>Body cell quantity(BCM)</TD><TD class=td align=middle>MyValue25
Kg</TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0><tr><td height=12></td></tr></table><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0><TR><TD></TD><TD>BMI--Body mass parameters， Also known as the body index,It is classified according to Asian standards：</TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE class=table cellSpacing=0 cellPadding=3 width=570 align=center border=0><TR class=td align=middle><TD class=td><STRONG>Low weight</STRONG></TD><TD class=td><STRONG>Standard   </STRONG></TD><TD class=td><STRONG>Overweight</STRONG></TD><TD class=td><STRONG>Pre-obesity</STRONG></TD><TD class=td><STRONG>Obesity Level 1</STRONG></TD><TD class=td><STRONG>Obesity Level 2</STRONG></TD><TD class=td><STRONG>Obesity Grade 3</STRONG>  </TD></TR><TR class=td align=left><TD class=td align=middle><18.5</TD><TD class=td align=middle>18.5~22.9</TD><TD class=td align=middle>≥23</TD><TD class=td align=middle>23~24.9</TD><TD class=td align=middle>25~29.9</TD>     <TD class=td align=middle>>30</TD><TD class=td align=middle>≥40</TD></TR><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0><tr><td height=12></td></tr></table><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0><TR><TD>   </TD><TD>BMR- Basal Metabolism (Unit: Calories)</TD></TR><TR><TD></TD><TD>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Basal metabolism refers to the energy metabolism of the human body under the basic state. The basic state refers to the state when a person is awake and very quiet, not affected by factors such as muscle activity, environmental temperature, food and mental stress. The basal metabolism within a unit of time is called the basal metabolic rate, which is the amount of heat dissipated per square meter of body surface per hour. It is considered normal if it does not exceed or fall below 15% of the normal value. The determination of basal metabolic rate is a major auxiliary method for the clinical diagnosis of thyroid diseases. When there is hyperthyroidism, the basal metabolic rate can increase significantly, while when there is hypothyroidism, it decreases significantly. Basal metabolic rate has a very significant impact on weight loss. Moderate exercise every day can help increase the body's basal metabolic rate, while dieting (at the extreme, fasting) can lower a person's basal metabolic rate. The basic agency rate can be roughly calculated through gender, age, height and weight. Measuring basal metabolic rate and energy metabolic rate under different activity intensities is also the basis for reasonably formulating nutritional standards and arranging people's diets.</TD></TR></TABLE> <TABLE width=590 align=center border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0><tr><td height=12></td></tr></table><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0><TR><TD><Font size=3>  <STRONG>  
4、Weight control</STRONG></Font></TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE class=table cellSpacing=0 cellPadding=3 width=590 align=center border=0><TR class=td align=middle bgcolor=B3D9ED height=28><TD class=td colspan=5>    <STRONG>Weight control</STRONG></TD></TR><TR class=td align=left bgcolor=EBF5FB height=32><TD class=td align=middle>Target weight</TD><TD class=td align=middle> MyValue26
Kg</TD></TR><TR class=td align=left bgcolor=EBF5FB height=32><TD class=td align=middle>Weight control</TD><TD class=td align=middle>MyValue27
Kg</TD></TR><TR class=td align=left bgcolor=EBF5FB height=32><TD class=td align=middle>Fat control</TD><TD class=td align=middle>MyValue28
Kg</TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0><tr><td height=12></td></tr></table><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0><TR><TD width=20></TD><TD> ①  Target weight: It is the standard weight calculated based on height.</TD></TR><TR><TD width=20></TD><TD> ② Weight control: It refers to the weight to be changed. A negative value indicates the need for reduction, while a positive value indicates the need for increase.</TD></TR><TR><TD width=20></TD><TD>③ Fat control: It refers to the amount of fat in the body that needs to be changed. A negative value indicates that it needs to be reduced (by doing aerobic exercise, improving metabolism, burning fat, and enhancing muscle strength), while a positive value indicates that it needs to be increased.</TD></TR></TABLE><TABLE width=590 align=center border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0><tr><td height=12></td></tr></table>      <TABLE width=590 align=center border=0><TR><TD><Font size=3><STRONG> 
